<h2>OB-GYN Emergencies — ภาวะฉุกเฉินสูติ-นรีวิทยา</h2>
<blockquote>"สูติศาสตร์ = high risk, high reward — ทำถูก = ช่วยชีวิต 2"</blockquote>
<h3>ภาพรวบ</h3>
<p>OB-GYN emergencies ที่ EMT พบ:</p>
<p>1. <strong>Normal delivery</strong> (ทารกคลอดที่เกิดเหตุ)</p>
<p>2. <strong>Obstetric complications</strong> (prolapsed cord, shoulder dystocia, breech)</p>
<p>3. <strong>Hemorrhage</strong> (ante/postpartum)</p>
<p>4. <strong>Eclampsia</strong> (ชักในตั้งครรภ์)</p>
<p>5. <strong>Gynecological emergencies</strong> (ectopic pregnancy, ovarian torsion)</p>
<h3>Normal Delivery (ทารกคลอดปกติ)</h3>
<h4>Signs of Impending Delivery</h4>
<ul><li>Contractions &lt;2 minutes apart</li><li>Crowning (หัวทารกเห็น)</li><li>Mother feels urge to push</li><li>Bulging perineum</li></ul>
<h4>Prehospital Delivery Procedure</h4>
<p>1. <strong>Scene safety + PPE</strong> (gloves, gown, eye protection)</p>
<p>2. <strong>Position mother</strong> — Supine on flat surface, knees bent</p>
<p>3. <strong>Prepare delivery kit</strong> — Clean towels, bulb syringe, clamps/cord ties, scissors</p>
<p>4. <strong>Support the head</strong> — As head crowns, apply gentle pressure (prevent explosive delivery)</p>
<p>5. <strong>Check for nuchal cord</strong> — Feel around neck for umbilical cord</p>
<ul><li>If present → gently slip over head, or clamp + cut if tight</li></ul>
<p>6. <strong>Deliver shoulders</strong> — Guide anterior shoulder down, then posterior shoulder up</p>
<p>7. <strong>Deliver body</strong> — Support the baby</p>
<p>8. <strong>Suction</strong> — Mouth first, then nose (bulb syringe)</p>
<p>9. <strong>Dry + warm</strong> — Dry vigorously, remove wet towel, place on mother's chest</p>
<p>10. <strong>Cord clamping</strong> — Clamp 2 places (15cm and 25cm from baby), cut between</p>
<p>11. <strong>Deliver placenta</strong> — Do NOT pull cord, allow spontaneous delivery (5-30 min)</p>
<p>12. <strong>Assess APGAR</strong> at 1 and 5 minutes</p>
<h4>APGAR Score</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>Component</th><th>0</th><th>1</th><th>2</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Appearance (สี)</strong></td><td>Blue/pale</td><td>Pink body, blue extremities</td><td>Completely pink</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Pulse (ชีพจร)</strong></td><td>Absent</td><td>&lt;100</td><td>&gt;100</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Grimace (reflex)</strong></td><td>No response</td><td>Grimace</td><td>Cry/cough</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Activity (การเคลื่อนไหว)</strong></td><td>Limp</td><td>Some flexion</td><td>Active motion</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Respiration (หายใจ)</strong></td><td>Absent</td><td>Slow/irregular</td><td>Strong cry</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Score 7-10 = Normal; 4-6 = Moderate depression; 0-3 = Severe depression</strong></p>
<h3>Complicated Deliveries</h3>
<h4>Prolapsed Cord (สายสื่อหลุด)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Umbilical cord delivers before the baby</strong></li><li><strong>EMERGENCY</strong> — cord compression → fetal hypoxia → death within minutes</li><li><strong>Signs:</strong> Cord visible/palpable in vagina</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong></li></ul>
<p>  1. <strong>Mother in Trendelenburg</strong> (head down) or knee-chest position</p>
<p>  2. <strong>Gently push presenting part UP</strong> off cord (do NOT let go)</p>
<p>  3. <strong>Keep cord warm</strong> (cover with moist gauze)</p>
<p>  4. <strong>Rapid transport to C-section</strong></p>
<p>  5. <strong>Mother pant</strong> (do NOT push)</p>
<ul><li><strong>Goal:</strong> C-section within 30 minutes</li></ul>
<h4>Shoulder Dystocia (ไหล่ติด)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Head delivers but shoulders get stuck</strong> behind pubic symphysis</li><li><strong>Signs:</strong> Turtle sign (head retracts), no progress despite pushing</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong></li></ul>
<p>  1. <strong>McRoberts maneuver</strong> — Hyperflex maternal thighs to abdomen (increases pelvic diameter)</p>
<p>  2. <strong>Suprapubic pressure</strong> — Push on baby's shoulder (NOT fundal pressure!)</p>
<p>  3. <strong>Woods screw rotation</strong> — Rotate baby's shoulder</p>
<p>  4. <strong>Delivery of posterior arm</strong></p>
<p>  5. ALL failed → <strong>Rapid transport</strong> (emergency C-section)</p>
<ul><li><strong>Complications:</strong> Brachial plexus injury (Erb's palsy), clavicle fracture</li></ul>
<h4>Breech Delivery (ทารกคลอดก้น)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Buttocks/feet deliver first</strong></li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> Head gets stuck, cord compression</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong></li></ul>
<p>  1. <strong>Support baby's body</strong> — Do NOT pull</p>
<p>  2. <strong>Allow gravity</strong> to help</p>
<p>  3. <strong>Lovset maneuver</strong> — Rotate baby to deliver shoulders</p>
<p>  4. <strong>Mauriceau-Smellie-Veit</strong> — Support baby's mouth with fingers, flex head</p>
<p>  5. If head stuck → <strong>Rapid transport</strong></p>
<h4>Nuchal Cord</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Cord wrapped around baby's neck</strong> (occurs in 20-30% of deliveries)</li><li><strong>Usually not dangerous</strong> — gently slip over head</li><li><strong>If tight</strong> → clamp cord in 2 places, cut between → deliver quickly</li></ul>
<h3>Postpartum Hemorrhage (PPH)</h3>
<h4>Definition</h4>
<ul><li><strong>&gt;500mL blood loss</strong> after vaginal delivery</li><li><strong>&gt;1000mL</strong> after C-section</li></ul>
<h4>Causes (4 T's)</h4>
<table>
<tr><th>T</th><th>Cause</th><th>Frequency</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Tone</strong></td><td>Uterine atony (most common, 70%)</td><td>⭐⭐⭐</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Trauma</strong></td><td>Lacerations, hematoma, uterine rupture</td><td>⭐⭐</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Tissue</strong></td><td>Retained placenta</td><td>⭐</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Thrombin</strong></td><td>Coagulopathy</td><td>⭐</td></tr>
</table>
<h4>Treatment</h4>
<p>1. <strong>Fundal massage</strong> — Massage uterine fundus (kneading motion) → stimulates contraction</p>
<p>2. <strong>Oxytocin</strong> 10-40U in 1L NS (ALS/hospital)</p>
<p>3. <strong>Breastfeeding/nipple stimulation</strong> → releases oxytocin</p>
<p>4. <strong>Rapid transport</strong></p>
<p>5. <strong>Treat shock</strong> — IV fluid, keep warm</p>
<p>6. <strong>Misoprostol</strong> (if no oxytocin available)</p>
<p>7. <strong>Tranexamic acid</strong> 1g IV (within 3 hours of delivery)</p>
<h4>Uterine Atony</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Uterus fails to contract</strong> after delivery → bleeding</li><li><strong>Signs:</strong> Boggy/non-palpable uterus, heavy vaginal bleeding</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Fundal massage, oxytocin, fluids, transport</li></ul>
<h3>Eclampsia (ชักในตั้งครรภ์)</h3>
<h4>Definition</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Pregnancy ≥20 weeks</strong> + <strong>Hypertension</strong> + <strong>Seizures</strong></li><li><strong>Pre-eclampsia</strong> = HTN + proteinuria/edema → may progress to eclampsia</li></ul>
<h4>Signs</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Hypertension</strong> (SBP &gt;140 or DBP &gt;90)</li><li><strong>Proteinuria</strong></li><li><strong>Edema</strong> (face, hands)</li><li><strong>Headache, visual changes, RUQ pain</strong></li><li><strong>Seizures</strong> (generalized tonic-clonic)</li></ul>
<h4>Treatment</h4>
<p>1. <strong>ABCs</strong> — Left lateral tilt, O2, airway protection</p>
<p>2. <strong>Magnesium sulfate 4g IV</strong> over 10-20 min → maintenance 1-2g/hr</p>
<p>3. <strong>Benzodiazepines</strong> if seizures continue</p>
<p>4. <strong>BP control</strong> — Hydralazine 5-10mg IV (target &lt;160/110)</p>
<p>5. <strong>Rapid transport</strong> — definitive treatment = delivery</p>
<p>6. <strong>Monitor</strong> — Fetal heart tones, maternal vital signs, repeat seizures</p>
<h3>Ectopic Pregnancy</h3>
<h4>Definition</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Embryo implants outside uterus</strong> (usually fallopian tube)</li><li><strong>Rupture</strong> → life-threatening hemorrhage</li><li><strong>Think ectopic</strong> in any woman of childbearing age with abdominal pain + shock</li></ul>
<h4>Signs</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Abdominal pain</strong> (usually unilateral)</li><li><strong>Vaginal bleeding</strong> (may be minimal)</li><li><strong>Signs of shock</strong> (tachycardia, hypotension, pallor)</li><li><strong>Positive pregnancy test</strong></li><li><strong>Adnexal tenderness</strong> on exam</li><li><strong>Referred shoulder pain</strong> (blood irritates diaphragm — Kehr's sign)</li></ul>
<h4>Risk Factors</h4>
<ul><li>Previous ectopic pregnancy</li><li>PID (pelvic inflammatory disease)</li><li>IUD</li><li>Tubal surgery</li><li>Smoking</li><li>Assisted reproduction</li></ul>
<h4>Treatment</h4>
<ul><li><strong>IV access</strong> — 2 large bore IV</li><li><strong>Fluid resuscitation</strong></li><li><strong>Rapid transport to OR</strong> (surgery = salpingectomy or salpingostomy)</li><li><strong>Do NOT delay</strong> — rupture can be fatal</li></ul>
<h3>Other Gynecological Emergencies</h3>
<h4>Ovarian Torsion</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Twisted ovary</strong> → ischemia</li><li><strong>Signs:</strong> Sudden unilateral lower abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, adnexal mass</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Pain management, rapid transport to OR</li></ul>
<h4>PID (Pelvic Inflammatory Disease)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Infection</strong> of upper genital tract (uterus, tubes, ovaries)</li><li><strong>Signs:</strong> Bilateral lower abdominal pain, fever, vaginal discharge, cervical motion tenderness</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Antibiotics (hospital), pain management</li></ul>
<h4>Vaginal Bleeding (Non-Pregnant)</h4>
<ul><li><strong>Causes:</strong> Menorrhagia, fibroids, cancer, trauma, coagulopathy</li><li><strong>Treatment:</strong> Shock management, rapid transport</li></ul>
<h3>Quiz</h3>
<details class="faq"><summary>Prolapsed cord — ทำอย่างไร?</summary><div class="body"><strong>Push presenting part UP off cord + Trendelenburg + rapid transport</strong> for C-section</div></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>Eclampsia — first-line anticonvulsant?</summary><div class="body"><strong>Magnesium sulfate 4g IV</strong> (NOT diazepam as first-line)</div></details>
<details class="faq"><summary>Ectopic pregnancy — think of it when?</summary><div class="body"><strong>Any woman of childbearing age + abdominal pain + shock</strong> — always consider ectopic!</div></details>
<h3>ภาษาไทย: OB-GYN Emergencies สำหรับ EMT</h3>
<ul><li><strong>Normal delivery</strong> — Support, don't pull, suction mouth first</li><li><strong>Prolapsed cord = push up + Trendelenburg</strong></li><li><strong>Shoulder dystocia = McRoberts + suprapubic pressure (NOT fundal)</strong></li><li><strong>Eclampsia = Magnesium sulfate IV</strong></li><li><strong>Ectopic pregnancy = always consider</strong> in women with abdominal pain + shock</li></ul>
